/**
 * Linux interview Q&A (DevOps / SRE / sysadmin, about 2 years of experience).
 * Same shape as the AI bank: { q, a, f:[[q,a]], probe, trap?, lv: "core" | "pro" | "tricky" }.
 */
window.LINUX_INTERVIEW = [
  {
    id: "internals", name: "Boot, kernel, processes and memory", icon: "fa-microchip",
    summary: "How the machine starts, how processes and memory really behave, and how to debug a stuck system.",
    topics: [{ id: "internals-q", t: "Questions", qa: [
      { lv: "core", q: "Explain the Linux boot process step by step, and where it can fail.", probe: "A clear mental model of boot stages and the recovery option at each.",
        a: "1. **Firmware (BIOS/UEFI)** runs self-tests and picks a boot device.\n2. **Bootloader (GRUB2)** loads from the disk/EFI partition, shows the menu, loads the kernel and initramfs.\n3. **Kernel** initialises hardware and memory, mounts the temporary root filesystem from the **initramfs** (which holds the drivers needed to find the real root: LVM, RAID, encryption, NVMe).\n4. The kernel mounts the real root and starts **PID 1** (systemd).\n5. **systemd** reads its default target (multi-user.target or graphical.target), mounts filesystems from /etc/fstab, and starts services in dependency order, then presents a login.\n\n**Failure points:** no bootable device (firmware settings), corrupt or missing GRUB (reinstall from a live CD or rescue mode), kernel panic or missing initramfs driver (boot an older kernel from the GRUB menu, regenerate with update-initramfs / dracut), root not found (wrong UUID or LVM not activated), a bad /etc/fstab entry dropping to emergency mode, or a failed service blocking a target.",
        f: [["What is the initramfs for?", "A small in-memory root with the drivers and tools needed to locate and mount the real root filesystem (storage controllers, LVM, RAID, LUKS). Without it a system whose root is on LVM or an encrypted disk could not boot."], ["How do you recover from a broken boot?", "Pick an older kernel in GRUB, or edit the entry (add systemd.unit=rescue.target or emergency.target, or rd.break to stop in initramfs), or boot a live image, chroot into the installed system and fix fstab/GRUB/initramfs."], ["Runlevels vs targets?", "systemd targets replace SysV runlevels: multi-user.target ≈ runlevel 3, graphical.target ≈ 5, rescue.target ≈ single-user. systemctl get-default and set-default control the default."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "A process is stuck in state D and kill -9 does nothing. What is happening and what do you do?", trap: "Weak answer: 'the process is a zombie' or 'use kill -9 harder'. D is a different state and signals are not delivered until the I/O returns.", probe: "Knowledge of process states and kernel I/O waits.",
        a: "State **D (uninterruptible sleep)** means the process is inside a kernel call waiting for I/O (a disk, a hung NFS server, a failing device, a stalled FUSE mount). Signals, including SIGKILL, are only delivered when the call returns, so the process cannot be killed until the I/O completes or fails. It is not a zombie (Z): zombies have already exited and are waiting for their parent to collect the status, and use no resources besides a process-table entry.\n\n**Investigate:** `ps -eo pid,stat,wchan:30,cmd | awk '$2 ~ /D/'` to see where it is waiting; `cat /proc/PID/stack` (root) for the kernel stack; `dmesg -T` for I/O errors, 'blocked for more than 120 seconds' hung-task messages or NFS 'server not responding'; `iostat -x` and `mount` to identify the device or share; `lsof -p PID` for the file involved.\n\n**Fix the cause, not the process:** restore the NFS server or network path, remove or replace the failing disk, or force an unmount (`umount -f`, `umount -l` lazy detach) so waiters get errors. Use soft/intr-style mount options where acceptable. As a last resort, reboot. Note that D-state processes still count toward load average, which is why load can be high with an idle CPU.",
        f: [["How does this affect load average?", "Tasks in D state are counted as runnable-ish in Linux load average, so a hung NFS mount can push load into the dozens while the CPU is idle."], ["What is a zombie and how do you remove it?", "A finished child whose parent has not called wait(). You cannot kill it; fix or restart the parent (or reparent to init by killing the parent) and the zombie disappears."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "Load average is 25 on an 8-core machine, but top shows the CPU mostly idle. How is that possible?", probe: "Understanding what load average measures.",
        a: "Load average counts tasks that are **runnable or in uninterruptible sleep (D)**, not only those using CPU. High load with idle CPUs means many tasks are blocked on I/O: slow or saturated disks, a hung network filesystem, or heavy swapping.\n\nTriage: `vmstat 1 5` (the `b` column is blocked tasks, `wa` is iowait, `si/so` swap activity), `iostat -x 1` (look for %util near 100 and high await on a device), `ps -eo state,pid,cmd | grep '^D'` to see the blocked processes, `dmesg -T` for hardware or NFS errors, `pidstat -d` to see which process is doing the I/O, and `free -h`/`vmstat` to rule out swap thrashing.\n\nThe opposite also occurs: load 4 on 4 cores with 100% CPU is a fully used but healthy machine. Compare load to core count, and use the 1/5/15-minute trend to tell rising from falling.",
        f: [["What would indicate memory pressure instead?", "Low available memory, non-zero swap in/out (si/so), kswapd using CPU, and eventual OOM-killer messages."], ["How do you find the process responsible?", "pidstat -d or iotop for I/O per process; ps sorted by state; lsof on the busy mount."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "Explain Linux memory reporting. Why is 'free' low but the system fine, how does the OOM killer choose, and how do you protect a critical process?", probe: "Real understanding of page cache and OOM behaviour.",
        a: "Linux uses spare RAM as **page cache** (file data) and buffers to speed up I/O, and gives it back when programs need memory. So `free` being small is normal. The number that matters is **available**: roughly free memory plus reclaimable cache. Worry when available is low and swap is actively used (`vmstat` si/so non-zero), not merely when free is low.\n\nWhen memory and swap are exhausted the **OOM killer** picks a victim by `oom_score` (visible in /proc/PID/oom_score), which grows with the process's memory use relative to total (plus adjustments). The kill is logged: `dmesg -T | grep -i 'killed process'`. The biggest consumer is chosen, not necessarily the leaking one.\n\n**Protection:** lower a critical process's score with `oom_score_adj` (-1000 exempts it; set via /proc/PID/oom_score_adj or `OOMScoreAdjust=` in the systemd unit); better, constrain the others with cgroup limits (`MemoryMax=`, `MemoryHigh=`) so a leaking service is killed first and the machine survives. Add swap as a buffer, fix the leak, and monitor available memory and swap.",
        f: [["Should you run `echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches` to 'free memory'?", "No: it only discards clean cache, hurts performance until the cache refills, and does not fix real shortage. It is useful only for benchmarking."], ["What is swappiness?", "A kernel setting (0-100, default 60) for how eagerly it swaps anonymous memory versus dropping cache; lower values prefer keeping applications in RAM, commonly 10 for database servers."]] },
      { lv: "tricky", q: "A service logs 'Too many open files'. Where are the limits, and why did your /etc/security/limits.conf change not work?", trap: "Weak answer: 'raise ulimit'. limits.conf does not apply to systemd services, which is the usual reason it 'does not work'.", probe: "File-descriptor limits at every level.",
        a: "There are several layers:\n- **Per-process soft/hard limit** (`ulimit -n`, shown in `/proc/PID/limits`, 'Max open files').\n- **Per-user/session limits** from PAM: /etc/security/limits.conf and limits.d, applied at login through pam_limits, so they affect interactive/SSH sessions, **not** services started by systemd.\n- **systemd services** take `LimitNOFILE=` in the unit (or a drop-in via `systemctl edit`), then `daemon-reload` and restart. The default is often 1024 soft, which is the usual culprit.\n- **System-wide**: `fs.file-max` and `fs.nr_open` (sysctl), and `cat /proc/sys/fs/file-nr` for current usage.\n\nDiagnose with `ls /proc/PID/fd | wc -l` versus the limit, `lsof -p PID | awk '{print $5}' | sort | uniq -c` to see what kind of descriptors (sockets, files, pipes), and check for leaks: a count that grows steadily means the application is not closing connections or files. Raising limits treats the symptom; a leak will hit the new limit later. Also consider ephemeral-port exhaustion and connection pooling.",
        f: [["How do you set it for a service?", "sudo systemctl edit myapp → add [Service] LimitNOFILE=65535, then systemctl daemon-reload and restart; verify with cat /proc/$(pidof myapp)/limits."], ["Soft vs hard limit?", "A process may raise its soft limit up to the hard limit; only root can raise the hard limit."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "What are namespaces and cgroups, and how do containers use them? How is a container different from a VM?", probe: "Container fundamentals at the kernel level.",
        a: "**Namespaces** isolate what a process can see: PID (own process tree), mount (own filesystem view), network (own interfaces, routes, ports), UTS (hostname), IPC, user (UID mapping) and cgroup namespaces. **cgroups** (control groups) limit and account for resources: CPU shares/quotas, memory, I/O, number of processes. A container is just a normal process (or tree) started inside a set of namespaces with cgroup limits and a root filesystem from an image; the runtime (containerd/runc) automates that.\n\nDifference from a VM: a VM runs its own kernel on virtualised hardware (strong isolation, more overhead, boots in seconds); a container shares the host kernel (light, starts in milliseconds, weaker isolation because a kernel bug or misconfiguration can affect the host). Security layers for containers: seccomp profiles, capabilities dropping, AppArmor/SELinux, rootless mode, read-only filesystems.\n\nYou can see it on any host: `lsns`, `ls -l /proc/PID/ns`, `systemd-cgls`, /sys/fs/cgroup, and `unshare` creates namespaces by hand.",
        f: [["What does a container memory limit do when exceeded?", "The kernel's cgroup OOM killer terminates a process inside that cgroup (the container), leaving the host alone; check `docker inspect` / dmesg for OOMKilled."], ["Why is running as root in a container risky?", "Container root is root on the shared kernel; a breakout or a mounted host path can reach the host. Use non-root users, drop capabilities and user namespaces."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "A process hangs or misbehaves with no useful logs. Walk me through debugging it with strace, lsof and /proc.", probe: "Practical live-debugging toolkit.",
        a: "1. **What is it doing?** `strace -f -tt -p PID` shows system calls with timestamps: a process stuck in `futex`, `read` on a socket, `connect` retrying, or `openat` returning ENOENT tells you where to look. `strace -c -p PID` summarises time per call. Use `-e trace=network` or `-e trace=file` to filter.\n2. **What does it have open?** `lsof -p PID` or `ls -l /proc/PID/fd`: sockets to which hosts, log files, deleted files, the working directory (`pwdx`).\n3. **State and resources:** `/proc/PID/status` (state, memory, threads), `/proc/PID/limits`, `/proc/PID/environ` (the environment it really runs with), `/proc/PID/cmdline`, `/proc/PID/stack` (kernel stack, root).\n4. **CPU spinning:** `top -H -p PID` per-thread usage, `perf top -p PID` or `perf record -g` for hot functions; `gdb -p PID` / `pstack` for a stack trace.\n5. **Network view:** `ss -tnp | grep PID`, `tcpdump` for the host in question.\n\nStrace slows the process noticeably, so use it briefly on production, with filters. For a crash, collect a core dump (`coredumpctl`) and the journal entries around it.",
        f: [["What would a process stuck in futex indicate?", "Waiting on a lock or condition variable, often a deadlock or a thread waiting for another that is blocked; take thread stacks with gdb to see which."], ["Why can strace be dangerous on production?", "It uses ptrace and can slow the target by an order of magnitude; use filters, short runs, or lighter tools such as perf, bpftrace or eBPF-based tracers."]] }
    ] }]
  },
  {
    id: "storage", name: "Filesystems, storage and permissions", icon: "fa-hard-drive",
    summary: "Links, inodes, deleted files, LVM, RAID, filesystem choices and permission design.",
    topics: [{ id: "storage-q", t: "Questions", qa: [
      { lv: "core", q: "Explain inodes, hard links and symbolic links. What happens when you delete a file that a process still has open?", probe: "Filesystem fundamentals that explain 'phantom' disk usage.",
        a: "An **inode** stores a file's metadata (owner, mode, size, timestamps, block locations) but not its name. A **directory entry** maps a name to an inode number. A **hard link** is another directory entry for the same inode (same data, cannot cross filesystems, cannot link directories); the file's data is freed only when its link count reaches zero **and** no process has it open. A **symbolic link** is a tiny file containing a path; it can cross filesystems and point to directories, and breaks (dangles) if the target moves.\n\nWhen you `rm` a file that a process still holds open, the directory entry is removed but the inode and blocks stay allocated until that process closes the file or exits. Result: `df` still shows the space as used while `du` cannot see it. Find such files with `lsof +L1` (link count below one), then restart the process, or reclaim immediately for an active log with `truncate -s 0 /proc/PID/fd/N` (carefully).",
        f: [["Why can't hard links cross filesystems?", "A hard link points to an inode number, and inode numbers are only unique within one filesystem."], ["What does `ls -i` show?", "The inode number; two names with the same number are hard links to the same file."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "df shows the filesystem 100% full but du on / adds up to much less. List the possible reasons and how you check each.", probe: "A classic that separates experience from memorisation.",
        a: "1. **Deleted but still open files:** `lsof +L1` or `lsof | grep deleted`; restart the process or truncate via /proc/PID/fd.\n2. **Files hidden under a mount point:** data written to a directory before another filesystem was mounted over it; `du` on the mount sees the new filesystem, the old data is invisible but still uses space. Check by unmounting briefly or bind-mounting the root (`mount --bind / /mnt/rootview; du -sh /mnt/rootview/*`).\n3. **Reserved blocks:** ext4 reserves ~5% for root by default, so `df` shows less free space than `du` explains for normal users (`tune2fs -l DEV | grep -i reserved`).\n4. **Inode exhaustion** reports a full disk with space left: `df -i`.\n5. **Sparse files and snapshots** (du reports allocated blocks; apparent sizes differ) and LVM/Btrfs/ZFS snapshots holding old data.\n6. **Different mounts counted:** du without `-x` crosses filesystems; use `du -xh --max-depth=1 /` to stay on one.\n7. **Journal and container layers:** /var/lib/docker, journald logs and package caches consuming space you did not look at.\n\nI check in that order, usually finding it in the first two.",
        f: [["How do you check for mount-shadowed data without unmounting production?", "Bind mount the parent filesystem to a temporary directory: the bind shows the underlying directory contents without the overlaid mount."], ["Can you free space from a deleted open file without restarting the process?", "Yes for logs: truncate the file through /proc/PID/fd/N (`: > /proc/PID/fd/N`), accepting that content is lost."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "Explain LVM and how you would grow a mounted filesystem online when the disk is almost full.", probe: "Storage administration you actually do in production.",
        a: "**LVM layers:** physical volumes (PVs: disks/partitions) → a volume group (VG: a pool) → logical volumes (LVs: the 'partitions' you put filesystems on). Benefits: resize online, span disks, snapshots, move data between disks (pvmove).\n\n**Grow an LV online:**\n1. Check free space: `vgs`, `lvs` (VFree in the VG). If none, add a disk: `pvcreate /dev/xvdf && vgextend vg0 /dev/xvdf`.\n2. Extend the LV and filesystem together: `lvextend -r -L +20G /dev/vg0/data` (or `-l +100%FREE`); the `-r` flag runs `resize2fs` (ext4) or `xfs_growfs` (XFS) for you.\n3. Verify: `df -h /data`, `lvs`.\n\nIf the underlying cloud disk was enlarged instead: `growpart /dev/xvda 1` (grow partition), `pvresize /dev/xvda1`, then lvextend -r. ext4 can grow online and (offline) shrink; **XFS can grow but never shrink**. Take a snapshot or backup before risky operations, and keep ~10-20% VG free for snapshots.",
        f: [["How do LVM snapshots work and what is the risk?", "Copy-on-write: the snapshot stores original blocks as the origin changes; if its allocated space fills, the snapshot becomes invalid. They are for short-term consistency (backups), not long-term retention."], ["Why use LVM on a cloud VM?", "Flexible growth and moving data between volumes without repartitioning; though many cloud setups simply grow the disk and the root filesystem."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "ext4 or XFS? And explain RAID 0, 1, 5, 6 and 10 with their trade-offs.", probe: "Design reasoning rather than definitions.",
        a: "**ext4:** mature, resizable both ways (shrinking offline), good general default, fsck tools. **XFS:** excellent for large files and parallel I/O, scales well to huge filesystems, fast mkfs and online growth, but cannot shrink and repairs use xfs_repair. RHEL defaults to XFS; Ubuntu to ext4. Btrfs/ZFS add snapshots and checksums when those matter.\n\n**RAID (mdadm or hardware):**\n- **0**: striping, speed and full capacity, no redundancy (one disk lost = all lost).\n- **1**: mirroring, survives all but one disk, 50% capacity, fast reads.\n- **5**: striping with single parity, survives 1 disk failure, capacity N-1; write penalty; long rebuilds risk a second failure on large disks.\n- **6**: double parity, survives 2 failures, capacity N-2, slower writes.\n- **10**: mirrors striped: good performance and redundancy, 50% capacity, the usual choice for databases.\n\nKey points: **RAID is not a backup** (it does not protect from deletion, corruption or ransomware); monitor degraded arrays (`/proc/mdstat`, `mdadm --detail`); test rebuild times; use hot spares; in the cloud, rely on the provider's replicated volumes plus snapshots.",
        f: [["Why is RAID 5 discouraged with large disks?", "Rebuilds read every remaining disk for hours or days, increasing the chance of a second failure or an unrecoverable read error before it completes."], ["How would you check array health?", "cat /proc/mdstat, mdadm --detail /dev/md0, and alerting from mdadm monitoring or SMART (smartctl) on the members."]] },
      { lv: "core", q: "A team needs a shared project directory where everyone can read and write each other's files. Design the permissions.", probe: "Practical use of groups, setgid and umask.",
        a: "1. Create a group and add the team: `groupadd project && usermod -aG project alice` (members re-login).\n2. Create the directory owned by that group: `mkdir /srv/project && chgrp project /srv/project`.\n3. Mode 2775: `chmod 2775 /srv/project`. The **setgid bit on the directory** makes new files and subdirectories inherit the group `project` instead of the creator's primary group.\n4. Make new files group-writable: the users' **umask should be 002** (or 0007 for private), set in their shell profile or via `pam_umask`; otherwise new files are created 644 and teammates cannot edit them.\n5. Optionally use default ACLs for stronger guarantees: `setfacl -d -m g:project:rwx /srv/project`.\n6. Consider the sticky bit (`chmod +t`) if members should not delete each other's files.\n\nTest by creating files as two users and checking `ls -l`; use `namei -l` if access fails to find a parent directory without execute permission.",
        f: [["Why is 'chmod 777' wrong here?", "It lets everyone (including service accounts and anyone with a login) modify and delete files; groups plus setgid give the needed collaboration without world access."], ["What does the sticky bit do?", "In a directory, only the file's owner (or the directory owner or root) may delete or rename entries, as in /tmp."]] },
      { lv: "tricky", q: "'I fixed it with chmod 777.' What does that hide, and how do you debug permission problems properly?", trap: "Weak answer: 'chmod 777 works, so it is fine'. It hides the real cause and creates a vulnerability.", probe: "Debugging method and security awareness.",
        a: "777 makes the file readable, writable and executable by everyone, so the access error disappears, but it also removes the protection that was working, makes the file modifiable by any account (including a compromised service), and hides the actual mistake (wrong owner, wrong user, missing directory execute bit, SELinux, read-only mount).\n\nProper debugging:\n1. **Who is running it?** `id`, or for a service `ps -o user,cmd -p PID` / `systemctl show -p User myapp`: services often run as www-data, postgres or a DynamicUser.\n2. **Look at the file and every directory on the path:** `namei -l /full/path/file`: a parent directory missing `x` for that user blocks everything below it.\n3. **Ownership and mode:** fix with `chown` to the right owner/group and the minimum mode (640/750), not world access.\n4. **ACLs and attributes:** `getfacl`, `lsattr` (immutable flag).\n5. **Mount options:** `findmnt -no OPTIONS /path` for ro or noexec.\n6. **Mandatory access control:** SELinux (`getenforce`, `ausearch -m avc`, `restorecon`) or AppArmor (`aa-status`, dmesg).\n7. **Systemd hardening:** ProtectSystem/ReadWritePaths in the unit can block writes even with correct file permissions.",
        f: [["What does `x` on a directory mean?", "Permission to traverse it: to access entries by name and cd into it. Without x on every parent directory the file is unreachable even if its own permissions allow it."], ["How does SELinux make Unix permissions 'wrong'?", "It adds a second check based on security contexts; an action can be denied even when mode and owner allow it. restorecon or a proper policy/boolean fixes it, not chmod."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "A bad /etc/fstab entry drops the server into emergency mode on boot. How do you recover and how do you prevent it?", probe: "Recovery procedure and defensive habits.",
        a: "**Recover:** at the emergency shell (give the root password) the root filesystem is mounted read-only: `mount -o remount,rw /`, then edit /etc/fstab (comment out or correct the bad line: wrong UUID, missing device, typo in type or options), run `mount -a` to test, then `systemctl daemon-reload` and `reboot`. If there is no root password or the system will not reach emergency mode, boot a rescue/live image, mount the root device, chroot and fix it, or use the cloud provider's recovery console.\n\n**Prevent:** always run `sudo mount -a` (and `findmnt --verify`) after editing fstab before rebooting; use UUIDs, not device names; add `nofail` (and `x-systemd.device-timeout=5s`) for non-essential or network mounts so boot continues without them; keep a console/serial or out-of-band access path; take a backup copy of fstab before editing; manage changes through configuration management and test them on a staging instance.",
        f: [["What does `nofail` do?", "Boot continues even if the device is absent or fails to mount instead of dropping to emergency mode."], ["Why prefer UUID over /dev/sdb1?", "Device names can change between boots with different enumeration order; UUIDs are stable identifiers of the filesystem."]] }
    ] }]
  },
  {
    id: "network", name: "Networking and DNS", icon: "fa-network-wired",
    summary: "From a name to a response: DNS, routing, TCP states, firewalls.",
    topics: [{ id: "network-q", t: "Questions", qa: [
      { lv: "pro", q: "What happens when you run `curl https://example.com` on a Linux host? Name where it can fail and which tool shows each failure.", probe: "End-to-end understanding of the network stack.",
        a: "1. **Name resolution:** the resolver follows /etc/nsswitch.conf (files → dns): /etc/hosts first, then DNS servers from /etc/resolv.conf or systemd-resolved (`resolvectl status`). Failures: 'Could not resolve host'. Tools: `getent hosts example.com` (what applications see), `dig example.com`, `dig @8.8.8.8 example.com` to compare resolvers.\n2. **Routing:** the kernel picks an interface and next hop from the routing table (`ip route get IP`); no default route = 'Network is unreachable'. For on-link hosts, **ARP** resolves the neighbour's MAC (`ip neigh`).\n3. **TCP handshake** (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) to port 443: refused (nothing listening, RST), timeout (firewall dropping or routing issue). Tools: `nc -zv host 443`, `curl -v`, `ss -tn`, `tcpdump`.\n4. **TLS handshake:** certificate validity, hostname match, trust chain, protocol/cipher support, SNI, clock skew. Tools: `openssl s_client -connect host:443 -servername host`, `curl -v` shows the verify error.\n5. **HTTP request/response:** status, redirects, headers, proxies (check `env | grep -i proxy`). Tool: `curl -sIL`.\n\nSystematic approach: test each layer in order and stop at the first failure.",
        f: [["Why check with `getent hosts` and not only dig?", "dig queries DNS directly and ignores /etc/hosts and nsswitch; getent uses the same path as applications, so it shows what programs really get."], ["A name resolves but curl times out on connect. What next?", "Test the port with nc -zv; compare local vs remote (firewall or security group); check the route and whether the service is listening on that interface (ss -tulpn)."], ["How do you debug a TLS error?", "openssl s_client to see the certificate chain and verification result; check the date (clock skew), the SNI name, missing intermediate certificates and the CA bundle."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "ss shows thousands of connections in TIME_WAIT (or CLOSE_WAIT). What does each mean and which one is a bug?", trap: "Weak answer: 'tune TCP settings'. CLOSE_WAIT piles are an application bug; TIME_WAIT is normal.", probe: "TCP state knowledge applied to real symptoms.",
        a: "**TIME_WAIT**: the side that closed the connection first (active closer) waits about 60 seconds to absorb stray packets and make sure the final ACK arrives. Large numbers are normal on busy clients/proxies that open many short connections. It is a problem only if you run out of ephemeral ports (`net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range`) or see 'cannot assign requested address'. Fixes: connection reuse (HTTP keep-alive, pooling), widening the port range, `tcp_tw_reuse` for outgoing connections, not aggressive tuning.\n\n**CLOSE_WAIT**: the remote side closed the connection, the kernel acknowledged, but **the local application has not closed its socket**. It does not time out by itself, so a growing CLOSE_WAIT count indicates a bug: a leaked connection or missing close() in the application (often in error paths or with connection pools). Find the process with `ss -tanp state close-wait` and fix the code or restart as a stopgap.\n\nOther states: SYN_RECV floods suggest a SYN flood (syncookies, rate limits); many ESTABLISHED to one backend may indicate a slow downstream.",
        f: [["Why can't you just disable TIME_WAIT?", "It protects against old duplicate packets corrupting a new connection with the same 4-tuple; shortening it trades safety for port reuse. Reusing connections is the right fix."], ["How would you count by state?", "ss -tan | awk 'NR>1 {print $1}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn"]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "Explain how iptables/nftables, ufw and firewalld relate, what connection tracking does, and how rule order matters.", probe: "Firewall architecture rather than ufw one-liners.",
        a: "**Netfilter** is the kernel packet-filtering framework. **iptables** (legacy) and **nftables** (modern, replaces it) are the user-space tools that program it; **ufw** (Ubuntu) and **firewalld** (RHEL) are friendlier front-ends that generate rules for you. Do not hand-edit iptables alongside ufw/firewalld: they will fight over the rules.\n\n**Chains and tables:** filter (INPUT, FORWARD, OUTPUT), nat (PREROUTING/POSTROUTING for DNAT, SNAT and masquerade), mangle, raw. Packets traverse chains top-down and the **first matching rule wins**; if none match the chain's **default policy** (ACCEPT or DROP) applies. So a broad ACCEPT above a specific DROP makes the DROP useless; insert deny rules above allows.\n\n**Connection tracking (conntrack)** makes the firewall stateful: a rule such as `-m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT` lets reply traffic through automatically, so you only write rules for new connections. The conntrack table has a size limit; a full table drops new connections (`nf_conntrack: table full`).\n\nStandard server policy: default deny inbound, allow ESTABLISHED/RELATED, allow SSH from known sources and the service ports required, log drops sparingly. Containers add their own chains (Docker manipulates iptables), so check `iptables -S` and `nft list ruleset` when behaviour is surprising.",
        f: [["DROP or REJECT?", "DROP silently discards (slows scanners, but clients time out); REJECT replies with an error (faster failure for legitimate clients). Many use DROP for the internet and REJECT internally."], ["How does port forwarding work?", "A DNAT rule in the nat table PREROUTING chain rewrites the destination, ip_forward must be enabled, and a masquerade/SNAT rule fixes the return path if needed."]] },
      { lv: "core", q: "How does DNS resolution work on a Linux client, and what is the order of precedence between /etc/hosts and DNS?", probe: "Basic client-side resolution.",
        a: "Applications call the C library resolver, which follows `hosts:` in **/etc/nsswitch.conf** (typically `files dns`, often with `resolve` or `mdns`). `files` means /etc/hosts first, so a static entry **overrides DNS**. Then DNS: the nameservers in /etc/resolv.conf (frequently a stub like 127.0.0.53 provided by systemd-resolved, with real upstream servers shown by `resolvectl status`), queried with a timeout and retries, applying `search` domains for short names. Results are cached by systemd-resolved, nscd or dnsmasq if present, and by the DNS servers according to TTL.\n\nTroubleshoot: `getent hosts name` (full application path), `dig name` / `dig @server name` (specific server), `resolvectl query name`, flush with `resolvectl flush-caches`, and remember that /etc/resolv.conf is usually managed by NetworkManager, netplan or systemd-resolved, so manual edits are overwritten.",
        f: [["What does a `search` domain do?", "Appends domains to short names: with `search corp.example`, `db1` is tried as db1.corp.example; it can cause extra queries and surprising matches."], ["Why does a stale /etc/hosts entry cause hard-to-diagnose bugs?", "Because it silently wins over DNS, so dig shows the right IP while applications connect to the old one."]] }
    ] }]
  },
  {
    id: "operations", name: "Services, logs and operations", icon: "fa-gears",
    summary: "systemd in depth, logging, zero-downtime deploys, scheduling and time.",
    topics: [{ id: "operations-q", t: "Questions", qa: [
      { lv: "pro", q: "Explain systemd dependencies and ordering (Requires, Wants, After, Before, BindsTo), restart policies, and how you would harden a service unit.", probe: "Real systemd fluency beyond start/stop.",
        a: "**Dependency vs ordering are separate:** `Wants=` is a soft dependency (start it too, but this unit runs even if it fails); `Requires=` is a hard dependency (if it fails to start, this unit fails; if it is stopped, this one is stopped); `BindsTo=` is stricter still (tied to its lifetime). None of these control order: `After=` and `Before=` do. Typical pair: `Requires=postgresql.service` plus `After=postgresql.service`. `WantedBy=multi-user.target` in [Install] decides what `enable` hooks it to.\n\n**Restart policy:** `Restart=on-failure` (or always), `RestartSec=5`, and rate limits `StartLimitIntervalSec/StartLimitBurst` to stop crash loops (the unit enters 'failed (start-limit-hit)' until `systemctl reset-failed`). `Type=` matters: `simple` (default, started immediately), `forking` (daemonises, needs PIDFile), `notify` (service signals readiness via sd_notify), `oneshot`.\n\n**Hardening options:** `User=`/`DynamicUser=yes`, `NoNewPrivileges=yes`, `ProtectSystem=strict` with `ReadWritePaths=`, `ProtectHome=yes`, `PrivateTmp=yes`, `PrivateDevices=yes`, `CapabilityBoundingSet=` (drop capabilities), `RestrictAddressFamilies=`, `MemoryMax=`, `LimitNOFILE=`, `SystemCallFilter=`. `systemd-analyze security myapp` scores a unit's exposure.\n\nEdit with `systemctl edit` (drop-ins survive package upgrades), then `daemon-reload` and restart.",
        f: [["What does `Type=notify` solve?", "systemd waits for the service to report readiness, so dependent units start only when it can actually serve, not merely when the process exists."], ["How does the environment differ from your shell?", "Services get a minimal environment; set variables with Environment= or EnvironmentFile=, not your .bashrc."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "A service starts and dies immediately and the log has nothing useful. What do you do?", probe: "Systematic debugging when the obvious has been tried.",
        a: "1. `systemctl status -l` and `journalctl -u svc -b --no-pager | head -50`: read the **first** error and exit code/signal (`status=203/EXEC` means the binary is not executable or not found; `217/USER` bad user; `200/CHDIR` working directory missing; `status=1/FAILURE` app error; `SIGSEGV` crash; `code=killed, signal=KILL` often the OOM killer).\n2. Run the command exactly as systemd would: `sudo -u svcuser env -i /path/to/bin args` or `systemd-run --uid=svcuser --pty /path/to/bin` to reproduce with the service user and a clean environment.\n3. Verify paths and permissions: absolute path in ExecStart, execute bit, the service user can read config and write its log/data directories (`namei -l`), SELinux denials (`ausearch -m avc -ts recent`).\n4. Config validation: `nginx -t`, `sshd -t`, the application's own check command; `systemd-analyze verify unit`.\n5. Resources and ports: disk full, memory (dmesg for OOM), port already in use (`ss -tulpn`).\n6. Increase verbosity: set the app's log level, `Environment=DEBUG=1`, or `strace -f` via `ExecStart=/usr/bin/strace -f -o /tmp/trace ...` temporarily.\n7. Check for crash dumps: `coredumpctl list`.\n\nKeep a note of what changed recently: package update, config change, rotated secret, new kernel.",
        f: [["What does status=203/EXEC mean?", "systemd could not execute the binary: wrong path, missing execute permission, bad shebang line or missing interpreter."], ["Why does it work from my shell but not as a service?", "Different user, PATH and environment, working directory, resource limits, and possible sandboxing options (ProtectSystem, PrivateTmp)."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "logrotate rotated the log but disk space was not freed and the application keeps writing to the old file. Explain and fix.", probe: "Understanding file handles and rotation modes.",
        a: "A process writes to an **open file descriptor** tied to the inode, not the name. If logrotate renames `app.log` to `app.log.1` (the default `create` mode) the process keeps writing to the renamed file; new logs are not in `app.log`, and if the old file is later deleted while still open its space is not released.\n\nOptions:\n- **Tell the app to reopen its log:** `postrotate` script sends a signal or reload (`systemctl reload nginx`, `kill -HUP $(cat /run/app.pid)`, `nginx -s reopen`). This is the clean solution.\n- **`copytruncate`:** copies the log and truncates the original in place, so the app needs no signal, at the cost of a small window where lines written between the copy and truncate are lost and extra I/O for large files.\n- Make the app log to **journald/syslog** and let it manage retention.\n\nAdd compression (`compress`, `delaycompress` if the app is slow to reopen), `missingok`, `notifempty`, size or time limits, and test with `logrotate -d` (debug) and `-f` (force). For immediate relief on a huge active log: `truncate -s 0 file`.",
        f: [["Why `delaycompress`?", "Skips compressing the most recent rotation for one cycle, which protects processes still writing to the file just rotated."], ["How would you find the files holding deleted space?", "lsof +L1; restart or signal those processes."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "How would you do a zero-downtime deployment on a single Linux host without containers or an orchestrator?", probe: "Practical deployment mechanics and rollback.",
        a: "**Layout:** each release in its own directory (`/opt/app/releases/20261029-1`), a symlink `/opt/app/current` pointing at the live one, shared data (uploads, config, logs) outside releases and linked in.\n\n**Procedure:**\n1. Build/copy the new release (rsync or a tarball), install dependencies, run migrations that are backward compatible with the old code.\n2. Start the new version on a **different port** (or via a second instance) and run health checks (`curl -f localhost:PORT/healthz`).\n3. Switch traffic atomically: update the symlink with `ln -sfn` (atomic rename) and **reload** (not restart) the process manager or proxy: `systemctl reload app` or nginx upstream switch; for processes that support it use graceful reload (SIGHUP/USR2 with Gunicorn, PM2 reload, nginx reload keeps connections draining).\n4. Keep the old release for **rollback**: point the symlink back and reload.\n5. Clean up old releases (keep the last 3-5).\n\nAlso: draining (stop accepting, finish in-flight requests), systemd socket activation to keep the listening socket open across restarts, database migrations in expand/contract steps so old and new code both work, and automated smoke tests after the switch. At larger scale, use a load balancer with rolling or blue/green deployments.",
        f: [["Why is `ln -sfn` atomic and `rm` then `ln` not?", "ln -sfn creates a temporary link and renames it over the old one (rename is atomic), so there is no instant when `current` does not exist."], ["What breaks zero-downtime most often?", "Incompatible database migrations, long-lived connections dropped on restart, and caches or sessions tied to the old version."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "Compare cron and systemd timers. When would you choose each?", probe: "Modern scheduling trade-offs.",
        a: "**cron:** simple, universal, one line per job, minimal environment, mails output by default, no built-in logging beyond syslog, no dependency handling, and missed runs are skipped (anacron handles that for desktops). **systemd timers** (a .timer unit that activates a .service): jobs are full services with logging in the journal (`journalctl -u job`), resource limits and sandboxing, dependencies and ordering, `Persistent=true` to run missed jobs after downtime, randomised delay (`RandomizedDelaySec`) to spread load, calendar expressions (`OnCalendar=*-*-* 02:00`), monotonic timers (`OnBootSec=`, `OnUnitActiveSec=`), and `systemctl list-timers` for visibility. They are more verbose (two files).\n\nChoose cron for a quick user-level job or portability; choose timers for system services where you want logs, limits, dependencies and missed-run handling. Either way: absolute paths, an explicit PATH, output captured, **flock** to prevent overlapping runs, time zone awareness (cron uses the server's), and idempotent scripts.",
        f: [["How do you prevent two runs overlapping?", "flock -n /run/job.lock cmd, or use a oneshot systemd service (a timer will not start a service already running)."], ["Why are `%` signs special in a crontab?", "They mean newline in the command field; escape them as \\\\%, for example in date +\\\\%F."]] },
      { lv: "core", q: "Why does time synchronisation matter on servers and how is it managed?", probe: "Awareness of a quiet source of outages.",
        a: "Wrong clocks break **TLS** (certificates appear expired or not yet valid), **Kerberos/SSO and JWT tokens** (skew limits of minutes), **log correlation** across hosts, scheduled jobs, database replication, distributed systems and anything with signatures or leases.\n\nManagement: a time daemon syncs the clock over NTP: `systemd-timesyncd` (simple SNTP client, default on many Ubuntu systems) or `chrony` (more accurate and robust, default on RHEL; better after suspend and on VMs). Check with `timedatectl` (System clock synchronized: yes, NTP service active), `chronyc tracking` and `chronyc sources`, set the time zone with `timedatectl set-timezone`, and keep servers in UTC. Never set the time by hand on a synchronised machine. In the cloud use the provider's NTP source (e.g. 169.254.169.123 on AWS); after a VM resume check for drift.",
        f: [["Step vs slew?", "A small offset is corrected by slewing (speeding up or slowing the clock) to keep time monotonic; large offsets are stepped (jumped), which can confuse applications, so chrony is configured to step only at start or above a threshold."], ["How would you detect drift fleet-wide?", "Export chrony/timedatectl offsets to monitoring and alert when the offset exceeds a few hundred milliseconds."]] }
    ] }]
  },
  {
    id: "scripting", name: "Shell scripting and text processing", icon: "fa-terminal",
    summary: "Writing scripts that survive real input and one-liners for log analysis.",
    topics: [{ id: "scripting-q", t: "Questions", qa: [
      { lv: "tricky", q: "What are the pitfalls of `set -e`, and what do you do instead of relying on it?", trap: "Weak answer: 'set -e makes scripts safe'. It has many exceptions and can mislead.", probe: "Depth of shell semantics.",
        a: "`set -e` exits when a command fails, but with notable exceptions: a failing command is **ignored** in conditions (`if cmd`, `while cmd`, `cmd && other`, `cmd || other`, `! cmd`); only the **last** command of a pipeline counts unless `set -o pipefail`; failures inside command substitutions in assignments are lost in some contexts (`local x=$(cmd)` masks the status; `x=$(cmd)` keeps it); functions called in a condition run with -e disabled; subshells inherit it inconsistently. It can also exit on harmless non-zero statuses such as `grep` finding nothing (status 1) or `((i++))` evaluating to zero.\n\nBetter practice: use `set -euo pipefail` as a baseline **and** handle expected failures explicitly (`grep -q ... || true`, `if ! cmd; then ...`), check critical steps with explicit tests and meaningful error messages, add `trap 'echo \"failed at line $LINENO\" >&2' ERR` for diagnostics and `trap cleanup EXIT` for temporary files, quote every expansion, validate arguments up front (`${1:?usage}`), prefer `[[ ]]` and `local` separation (`local x; x=$(cmd)`), run `shellcheck` in CI, and test failure paths. For anything non-trivial consider Python.",
        f: [["Why does `local x=$(false)` not trigger -e?", "The exit status of the `local` builtin (0) replaces the substitution's status; declare then assign separately."], ["What does pipefail change?", "The pipeline's status becomes that of the last command that failed (non-zero) instead of just the final command, so `cmd1 | cmd2` fails if cmd1 fails."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "Why is `for f in $(ls *.txt)` wrong, and what are the correct ways to loop over files and to handle arbitrary file names?", probe: "Word splitting, globbing and safe iteration.",
        a: "`$(ls ...)` produces a string that the shell **word-splits on whitespace** (and globs again), so a file named `my report.txt` becomes two items, names with newlines or leading dashes break, and ls output is for humans, not scripts.\n\n**Correct patterns:**\n- Let the shell glob: `for f in *.txt; do [ -e \"$f\" ] || continue; echo \"$f\"; done` (guard for the no-match case; set `shopt -s nullglob` alternatively).\n- Recursive and safe for any name: `find . -type f -name '*.txt' -print0 | xargs -0 -n1 cmd` or `find ... -exec cmd {} +`, or `while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do ...; done < <(find ... -print0)`.\n- Always **quote** `\"$f\"`; use `--` to end options when names may start with `-` (`rm -- \"$f\"`).\n- Arrays for lists: `files=(*.txt); cmd \"${files[@]}\"`.\n- Read lines safely: `while IFS= read -r line; do ...; done < file`.\n\nNames can contain spaces, newlines, quotes, leading dashes and non-UTF-8 bytes; NUL (`-print0`/`-0`) is the only byte that cannot appear in a path.",
        f: [["What does an unquoted `$var` do?", "Undergoes word splitting and globbing; an empty variable disappears entirely (`rm $dir/*` becomes `rm /*`), so quote variables."], ["Why `IFS= read -r`?", "IFS= keeps leading and trailing whitespace; -r stops backslash interpretation."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "From an nginx access log, list the top 10 client IPs that produced 5xx responses, and explain each stage. How do you make it work on gigabytes of compressed logs?", probe: "Fluent text pipelines and awareness of performance.",
        a: "```\nawk '$9 ~ /^5[0-9][0-9]$/ {print $1}' access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -10\n```\n- `awk '$9 ~ /^5../'`: in the common/combined log format field 1 is the client IP and field 9 the status; the regex keeps 500-599. Check your own `log_format`: field positions differ.\n- `{print $1}`: output only the IP.\n- `sort`: required because `uniq` only collapses **adjacent** duplicates.\n- `uniq -c`: count per IP. `sort -rn`: numerically, largest first. `head -10`.\n\nAlternatively one awk pass: `awk '$9 ~ /^5/ {c[$1]++} END {for (ip in c) print c[ip], ip}' access.log | sort -rn | head`, which avoids sorting millions of lines.\n\n**Large/compressed logs:** `zcat access.log*.gz | awk ...` (or `zgrep`), process rotated files together, use `LC_ALL=C sort` (much faster), `mawk`/`gawk`, `pigz -dc` for parallel decompression, `GNU parallel` or split by file for multiple cores, or load into a proper log tool. Extend with time windows (`awk -v since=...` or filter on the timestamp field) and per-URL breakdowns.",
        f: [["How do you restrict to the last hour?", "Filter by timestamp field converted to epoch, or grep for the hour string ('29/Oct/2026:14:') for a quick approximation; for precision parse with awk's mktime or use a log tool."], ["Why LC_ALL=C for sort?", "Avoids locale-aware collation, which is much slower; byte order is enough for counting."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "Subshell vs `source` vs `exec`: explain, and say why variables set inside `cmd | while read ...` disappear.", probe: "Understanding process boundaries in the shell.",
        a: "- **Running a script** (`./script.sh`, `bash script.sh`) starts a **child process**: its variables, `cd`, and `export` changes vanish when it ends; only exported variables flow **into** it, never back.\n- **`source script.sh` (or `. script.sh`)** runs the commands in the **current shell**, so variables, functions and `cd` persist. That is how `.bashrc` and virtualenv `activate` work.\n- **`exec cmd`** replaces the current shell process with `cmd` (no return); used in wrapper scripts and container entrypoints so signals go straight to the real process (PID 1 handling).\n- **Subshells** are created by `( ... )`, command substitution `$( ... )`, background `&` and each stage of a **pipeline**.\n\nSo in `cat file | while read x; do count=$((count+1)); done`, the loop runs in a subshell (the right side of a pipe), and `count` is lost afterwards. Fixes: redirect instead of piping (`while read x; do ...; done < file`), or process substitution (`while read x; do ...; done < <(cmd)`), or `shopt -s lastpipe` in scripts. This is also why `cd` inside `( )` or a script does not change your terminal's directory.",
        f: [["Why use `exec` in a Docker entrypoint script?", "So the application becomes PID 1 and receives SIGTERM from the container runtime directly instead of a shell that may not forward it."], ["How does `export` interact with child processes?", "Exported variables are copied into the child's environment at start; later changes in either process do not affect the other."]] },
      { lv: "tricky", q: "What is the difference between `cmd > file 2>&1` and `cmd 2>&1 > file`?", trap: "Weak answer: 'they are the same'. Redirections are processed left to right.", probe: "Precise understanding of file descriptor duplication.",
        a: "Redirections are applied **left to right**, and `2>&1` means 'make fd 2 a copy of whatever fd 1 points to **right now**'.\n\n- `cmd > file 2>&1`: first stdout goes to the file, then stderr is duplicated onto stdout's destination, so **both** go to the file.\n- `cmd 2>&1 > file`: first stderr is duplicated onto stdout's current destination (the terminal or pipe), **then** stdout is redirected to the file; so stdout goes to the file but stderr still goes to the terminal (or the pipe).\n\nThe second form is sometimes used deliberately to pipe only stderr: `cmd 2>&1 >/dev/null | grep error`. In bash, `&> file` is shorthand for the first form. In scripts also be careful with `sudo cmd > file` (the redirect runs as you, not root): use `cmd | sudo tee file`.",
        f: [["How do you discard stdout but keep stderr on screen?", "cmd >/dev/null (stderr is untouched)."], ["How do you capture both into a variable?", "out=$(cmd 2>&1) mixes them; to separate, redirect one to a file or use process substitution."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "How do you avoid race conditions in scripts that create temporary files, run concurrently or write config files?", probe: "Safe scripting for production.",
        a: "- **Temporary files:** never use predictable names like `/tmp/out.$$` (symlink attacks and collisions). Use `tmp=$(mktemp)` (or `mktemp -d`), set `trap 'rm -rf \"$tmp\"' EXIT` for cleanup.\n- **Mutual exclusion:** `flock -n /run/myjob.lock cmd` (advisory lock released automatically on exit/crash), or `exec 9>/run/job.lock; flock -n 9 || exit 0` inside the script. Avoid 'check if file exists then create it' lock files, which are not atomic and survive crashes.\n- **Atomic updates:** write to a temp file on the **same filesystem**, `fsync` if needed, then `mv` it over the target: rename is atomic, so readers see either the old or the new file, never a half-written one (`mv -f \"$tmp\" /etc/app.conf`).\n- **Idempotency:** scripts should be safe to re-run (`mkdir -p`, `ln -sfn`, check-then-act with locks).\n- **Time-of-check/time-of-use:** do not test a path then act on it; act and handle the error, or use `open` with exclusive-create semantics (`set -o noclobber` / `>|`).\n- **Cron overlap:** use flock or a systemd oneshot service.",
        f: [["Why must the temp file be on the same filesystem as the target?", "rename() is atomic only within one filesystem; across filesystems mv copies and deletes, which is not atomic."], ["What is noclobber?", "`set -o noclobber` makes `>` refuse to overwrite an existing file (use `>|` to force), a cheap guard against accidental truncation and a way to create files exclusively."]] }
    ] }]
  },
  {
    id: "security", name: "Security and incident response", icon: "fa-lock",
    summary: "Hardening, auditing, sudo design and responding to a suspected compromise.",
    topics: [{ id: "security-q", t: "Questions", qa: [
      { lv: "pro", q: "You are handed a fresh internet-facing Ubuntu server. What do you do in the first hour to harden it, in order?", probe: "A prioritised, practical hardening checklist.",
        a: "1. **Update:** `apt update && apt full-upgrade`, enable **unattended-upgrades** for security patches.\n2. **Accounts:** create a named admin user with sudo; no direct root login; remove default or unused accounts; lock the root password.\n3. **SSH:** keys only (`PasswordAuthentication no`), `PermitRootLogin no`, `AllowUsers`/`AllowGroups`, modern algorithms; test with `sshd -t` and a second session before reloading; optionally move behind a bastion/VPN or restrict source IPs.\n4. **Firewall:** `ufw default deny incoming`, allow only SSH (from known sources if possible) and required service ports, then enable.\n5. **Reduce attack surface:** `ss -tulpn` to list listening services; disable or remove what is unused (`systemctl disable --now`), uninstall unnecessary packages.\n6. **Brute-force protection:** fail2ban (or rely on key-only + firewall).\n7. **Least privilege:** dedicated service accounts, narrow sudoers entries, file permissions 640/750, secrets outside the repo with mode 600; systemd sandboxing options for services.\n8. **Logging and monitoring:** persistent journald, forward logs off-box, auditd for sensitive paths, alerts on logins, sudo use and config changes.\n9. **Integrity and baseline:** AIDE or similar, CIS benchmark (Lynis scan), kernel hardening sysctls, disable unused filesystems/protocols.\n10. **Backups and recovery:** tested backups, snapshots, documented rebuild; treat servers as replaceable via configuration management.\n\nKeep a console path so a firewall or SSH mistake does not lock you out.",
        f: [["Why change the SSH port?", "It only reduces log noise from bots; it is not security by itself. Key-only authentication and firewalling are the real controls."], ["What is the order risk with ufw?", "Enabling the firewall over SSH before allowing port 22 locks you out; allow first, then enable."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "You suspect a Linux server has been compromised. How do you investigate, contain and recover?", probe: "Incident response method, evidence handling and the 'rebuild' mindset.",
        a: "**Principles:** preserve evidence, avoid tipping off or destroying data, assume the attacker can see what you run, and prefer rebuilding over cleaning.\n\n**1. Contain without destroying:** isolate the host from the network (security group/VLAN) rather than powering off (memory state is lost); snapshot the disk and, if possible, memory; keep a timeline.\n**2. Gather volatile data first:** `who`, `w`, `last -a`, `lastb`; running processes `ps auxf` (odd parents, deleted binaries: `ls -l /proc/*/exe | grep deleted`); network connections `ss -tunap` and listening ports; open files `lsof`; loaded modules `lsmod`; environment of suspicious PIDs.\n**3. Persistence checks:** `crontab -l` for every user and /etc/cron*, systemd units and timers (`systemctl list-unit-files`, recent files in /etc/systemd/system), `~/.ssh/authorized_keys` for all users, `/etc/ld.so.preload`, shell profiles and rc files, new users/UID 0 accounts (`awk -F: '$3==0' /etc/passwd`), SUID changes (`find / -perm -4000`), sudoers.d.\n**4. Logs and integrity:** auth.log/secure, journald, web logs for the initial vector; recently modified files (`find / -xdev -mtime -2 -type f`); package integrity `debsums -c` / `rpm -Va`; compare against a known-good baseline.\n**5. Eradicate and recover:** identify the entry point (vulnerable service, stolen key, weak password) and close it; **rebuild from a trusted image** and restore data from a pre-compromise backup, rather than trusting a cleaned system; **rotate every credential and key** that was on or accessible from the host; patch; add detection.\n**6. Report and learn:** document, notify per policy, post-mortem.",
        f: [["Why not just delete the malware and move on?", "You cannot be sure what else changed (rootkits, backdoors, altered binaries), and the original weakness remains; a rebuild gives a known state."], ["Why avoid rebooting first?", "Volatile evidence (processes, connections, memory) is lost, and some malware cleans up on shutdown."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "How do you design sudo access safely, and why are NOPASSWD and wildcards in sudoers dangerous?", probe: "Privilege management details.",
        a: "**Principles:** named users via groups (`%ops`), not shared accounts; the narrowest commands that do the job; separate files in `/etc/sudoers.d/` edited with `visudo -f`; keep `env_reset` and `secure_path`; log everything (sudo logs to auth.log/journal, optionally `log_output` for sessions); require a password for broad rights.\n\n**NOPASSWD** removes the second factor between an unprivileged compromise and root: any process or stolen session running as that user can use it. Acceptable for a **specific**, harmless command (for example restarting one service for a deploy account): `deploy ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl restart myapp`; dangerous as `NOPASSWD: ALL`.\n\n**Wildcards and escapes:** an allowed command with `*` can be abused (`/bin/cat /var/log/*` lets someone read `/var/log/../../etc/shadow`; `tar`, `rsync`, `find`, `vim`, `less`, `awk`, `git` and many others can run a shell or arbitrary commands: 'shell escapes', see GTFOBins). Also arguments: allowing `systemctl *` permits `systemctl edit` or starting arbitrary units; allowing an editor gives a root shell. Fixes: allow exact commands with exact arguments, wrapper scripts owned by root that validate input, `sudoedit` for file editing (edits a copy as the user), and the `NOEXEC` tag to block escapes from pagers/editors.\n\nReview with `sudo -l -U user`, audit changes, and prefer role-based access plus temporary elevation for rare tasks.",
        f: [["Why use `sudoedit` instead of `sudo vim`?", "It opens a copy as your own user with your editor settings and writes it back as root, so the editor never runs privileged and cannot spawn a root shell."], ["What does `secure_path` do?", "Forces a trusted PATH for commands run through sudo, preventing a user-controlled PATH from substituting binaries."]] }
    ] }]
  },
  {
    id: "scenarios", name: "Scenario walk-throughs", icon: "fa-triangle-exclamation",
    summary: "Open-ended incidents where the interviewer wants to hear your method.",
    topics: [{ id: "scenarios-q", t: "Questions", qa: [
      { lv: "pro", q: "Production server: SSH has stopped responding, but the website it hosts still works. What do you do?", probe: "Calm, structured diagnosis with minimal risk.",
        a: "The site working proves the machine, network path to the web ports and kernel are alive, so the issue is specific to SSH or access to it.\n\n1. **From my side:** `ssh -vvv user@host` to see where it stalls (TCP connect, key exchange, auth, session); `nc -zv host 22` to distinguish refused (daemon down or wrong port) from timeout (firewall/security group changed, source IP blocked, rate limiting); try from another network/IP to rule out a local ISP or my own address being banned (fail2ban).\n2. **Check what changed recently:** firewall/security-group edits, a config-management run, sshd_config change, a package update, my IP changing.\n3. **Alternative access:** cloud console / serial console / SSM session / out-of-band management, or another admin path (bastion, VPN).\n4. **On the host:** `systemctl status ssh`, `journalctl -u ssh -n 100`, `sshd -t`, `ss -tlnp | grep :22`, `ufw status`/`iptables -S`, `df -h` and inodes (a full disk breaks logins), memory/OOM (`dmesg`), `ulimit`/PAM issues, `/var/log/auth.log`, fail2ban bans (`fail2ban-client status sshd`), and hanging login causes: DNS reverse lookups (`UseDNS`), slow PAM modules, a huge or broken /etc/motd scripts, NFS home directories.\n5. **Fix:** restart sshd only after `sshd -t` passes (existing sessions survive a restart of the daemon), unban the IP, correct the firewall rule, free disk.\n6. **Prevent:** monitoring on port 22 and on login latency, break-glass access documented, config in version control.",
        f: [["Why does a login 'hang' after the password is accepted?", "Usually slow PAM modules, reverse DNS lookups (UseDNS), a stuck NFS home directory, or heavy login scripts; ssh -vvv shows the stage and the server's auth log shows the delay."], ["Why is restarting sshd usually safe?", "Established sessions are separate child processes and continue; but validate the config first, because a broken config prevents the new daemon from starting."]] },
      { lv: "pro", q: "Explain the USE method and how you would apply it to a slow Linux server.", probe: "A framework for performance analysis, not a bag of commands.",
        a: "The **USE method** (Brendan Gregg) says: for every **resource** check **Utilisation** (percent of time busy), **Saturation** (queued work beyond capacity) and **Errors**. It prevents randomly staring at top.\n\nResources and tools:\n- **CPU:** utilisation `mpstat -P ALL 1`, `top`; saturation: load average above core count, run queue `vmstat 1` column r; errors: rare (machine-check events in dmesg).\n- **Memory:** utilisation `free -h` (available), saturation: swapping (`vmstat` si/so), page-scan activity, OOM kills; errors: allocation failures, ECC errors in dmesg.\n- **Disk:** utilisation `iostat -x` (%util), saturation: average queue size and await, blocked tasks (`vmstat` b, D state); errors: dmesg I/O errors, SMART.\n- **Network:** utilisation: interface throughput vs link speed (`sar -n DEV 1`, `ip -s link`), saturation: drops, retransmits and backlog (`ss -s`, `nstat`, `ip -s link` drops), errors: interface error counters.\n- **Software resources:** file descriptors, threads/processes, connection pools, locks (`ulimit`, `lsof | wc`, app metrics).\n\nProcedure: list resources, check U/S/E for each quickly, and follow the one that is saturated; then go deeper with per-process tools (pidstat, perf, strace). Pair it with the **RED** method (Rate, Errors, Duration) for services.",
        f: [["Why is saturation often more telling than utilisation?", "A resource at 70% utilisation with nothing queued is fine, while a resource at 100% with a growing queue is where latency comes from."], ["Where do you start for a slow web request?", "RED metrics on the service to see errors and latency, then USE on the host and its dependencies (database, disk, network) to find the saturated resource."]] }
    ] }]
  }
];
